{
  "result": "There's a particular kind of silence that lives between five and six in the morning. It's not the heavy silence of midnight, but a thin, expectant hush. The world hasn't decided to wake up yet. \n\nI like that hour. The light is blue-grey, and everything feels slightly unreal, like you're seeing the set before the play begins. The kitchen is cold. The kettle takes its time to boil, and that small sound fills the whole house. \n\nWe spend so much of our lives trying to be efficient, to optimize, to squeeze more into each day. We track our steps, our sleep, our productivity. And yet, the most memorable moments are almost always inefficient. A long conversation that goes nowhere and everywhere. Getting lost on a walk and finding a street you've never seen before. Sitting with a book for an hour and only reading ten pages because you kept staring out the window, thinking. \n\nI think we forget that we are not machines designed for output, but animals designed for noticing. Our eyes are built to catch movement in leaves, our ears to find patterns in birdsong, our hands to know the weight of a stone or the warmth of a mug. \n\nMaybe the point isn't to do more, but to notice more. To collect small, useless beauties: the way light falls across a wooden floor, the smell of rain on hot pavement, the perfect, brief steam rising from tea. \n\nIn the end, those are the things we keep forever.",
